# Warren threat model

Status: written 2026-10-22 against `main` at `de49e96` (PR #36).
Review this whenever a runtime, credential path, or route that touches a workspace changes.

## Premise: the agent is hostile

Warren runs coding agents (Claude Code and, later, other harnesses) inside workspaces.
The design assumes the agent and everything it can write are hostile. A prompt injection
in a repo, issue, web page or dependency can make an otherwise well-behaved agent act
against its operator. Treat the following as attacker-controlled:

- every process inside the workspace, and anything they can spawn;
- every file the workspace can write: the checkout, `.git/`, rc files, harness state, and
  anything on the workspace's EFS access point, from earlier task launches too;
- project and template config once it has been persisted and read back (`spec_json`),
  because it may predate a validator.

Warren's own processes are trusted: the CLI on the user's host, the control plane
(`warren-server`), and the materialization step that runs before the agent.

## Assets

| Asset | Where it lives | Who may hold it |
|---|---|---|
| Host filesystem outside the workspace (`~`, `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, other repos) | user's machine / podman host | user only |
| Host harness config (`~/.claude`, settings, credentials) | user's machine | user; workspace gets scoped, mostly read-only views |
| Project secrets (`secrets.env`, warren-server's encrypted store) | host / control plane | only workspaces whose template declares them |
| GitHub App private key | OS credential store (local issuer) or Secrets Manager (control-plane issuer) | the issuer only; never a workspace |
| GitHub installation tokens | minted per workspace, 1 hour | that workspace's git credential helper |
| `git_write` GitHub tokens (push + PRs) | minted by the control plane per running `git_write: true` workspace, 1 hour, refreshed, revoked on stop/destroy; `/run/warren-git/<owner>` in the container (not the volume) | that workspace's agent, for its template's writable repos only |
| git-crypt key | operator | materialization step only, and only with `git_crypt: unlock_and_drop` |
| AWS credentials | host SSO session; ECS task roles | template's single profile (local `export` mode); the minimal task role (ECS) |
| Model access (Bedrock today) | AWS credentials / proxy secret | the harness, scoped to specific models |
| Operator's Claude login (OAuth tokens) | host Keychain / `~/.claude/.credentials.json` | the operator; a subscription-mode workspace gets a per-launch copy of the access token only, a Bedrock-mode workspace gets nothing |
| Control-plane API tokens | CLI config, server DB | users; workspace-scoped machine tokens for agents |
| Other users' workspaces and secrets | control plane, ECS, EFS | their owner |
| Control-plane IAM role and infrastructure | ECS service, IaC | the control plane only (#3) |
| Audit trail | structured logs (`event="audit"`) | read by operators; written only by the control plane |

## Trust boundary per runtime

| Runtime | Boundary | Strength | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| **seatbelt** (macOS, `sandbox-exec`) | SBPL profile generated by warren, invoked by absolute path (AE2), profile and state kept outside the workspace | **Mistake containment, not a security boundary** | `lsopen`, `mach-lookup`/`mach-register`, `iokit-open` and `distributed-notification-post` stay open (see [SECURITY.md](../SECURITY.md#known-limitations)); full network. Do not run an untrusted agent here expecting host isolation. |
| **bubblewrap** (Linux) | user/pid/uts/ipc namespaces, bind mounts limited to the workspace and declared paths, `--die-with-parent` | Medium | Network is shared with the host (`--share-net`); same kernel. |
| **nsjail** (Linux) | namespaces including a fresh, interface-less network namespace (`clone_newnet`), seccomp policy, config regenerated from the persisted spec into warren's state dir on every connect | Medium–high | Same kernel; no network unless configured. |
| **docker / podman** | container: separate mount/pid/net namespaces, bind mounts only for the workspace and declared folders (`ro` where declared); server-side podman is rootless, in a `keep-id:uid=1000,gid=1000` user namespace by default (`WARREN_PODMAN_USERNS`) | Medium–high | keep-id maps the server's host user to container uid 1000 (`warren`), so the bind mounts are `warren`'s inside and what `warren` writes is the server user's on the host. This is no more host privilege than podman's default mapping, where the server user is container root: container root and every other container uid are still unprivileged subuids on the host, and SSH logins as root stay disabled. With `warren-docker-base` the workload (tmux, harnesses, agent supervisor, app) now runs as that uid 1000 rather than container root, after a short root phase in the entrypoint (writes `/etc/warren/*`, then drops): a non-root workload can no longer change root-owned files in the image or install packages, and its files in the checkout belong to the server user instead of a subuid, so the server's host-side git operations and cleanup need no `podman unshare` workaround. What doesn't change: the workload can still write everything the server user can reach through the bind mounts (root inside the container could already do that under the default mapping), container root is still an unprivileged subuid on the host, and the drop depends on the mount owner (it stays root under `host`, and under rootful docker unless the server's uid is 1000, where Docker's root is host root). `WARREN_PODMAN_USERNS=host` restores the default mapping. Docker is rootful and doesn't use it. No east-west path between workspaces: each podman workspace has its own `isolate`d bridge (`<egress_bridge_name>-<id>`, used for egress byte counting, removed on destroy, orphans pruned at startup, workspaces still on the old shared bridge migrated at startup); docker workspaces join `warren-isolated`, a bridge with inter-container communication off. The server refuses to start on rootless podman with the pasta network helper (host loopback reachable from workspaces) unless `WARREN_PODMAN_ALLOW_PASTA=1`; see residual risk 6. A podman workspace's SSH port is published on `127.0.0.1` only (`WARREN_SSH_PUBLISH_HOST`), and each port a template declares under `ports:` is published on host loopback on an ephemeral host port (podman and docker; never `0.0.0.0`, even when `WARREN_SSH_PUBLISH_HOST` is wider) for `warren proxy`. Containers can't reach host loopback under rootless podman with slirp4netns, so other workspaces can't reach these ports; see residual risk 6 for pasta and desktop VMs. Docker workspaces publish only declared ports. Outbound traffic is unrestricted. Templates' `docker_networks` attach extra, shared networks by design. |
| **ECS Fargate** | **the task itself.** Firecracker micro-VM per task, minimal task role, EFS access point per workspace | High against the host; **none inside the task** | See the residual risks below. |

### Orchestrator → workspace traffic

The orchestrator never dials the sidecar. All orchestrator→workspace traffic uses the
runtime's own authenticated control channel: podman API exec or the ECS Exec relay,
container logs, and host-side state files read without following links. The sidecar is
only an in-container agent-state recorder: it tails the tmux pane for the OSC 7501 status
escape and writes `osc-state.json` under the harness root, and it has no socket or port.
That file is agent-writable like the rest of the harness state, so the server parses it
as untrusted (size-capped, no-follow read, schema-checked). If the orchestrator ever needs
structured data from inside a workspace, it runs a command through the control channel
and reads the output. No runtime has an inbound network listener on a workspace.

### ECS Fargate

Verified 2026-10-18 on platform 1.4.0: `unshare -U`, `bwrap` and every `--unshare-*` flag
fail with `Operation not permitted`, `SYS_ADMIN` is rejected at `RegisterTaskDefinition`,
and `NET_ADMIN` is unavailable. There is no in-task sandbox, and no way to hide the
credentials endpoint `169.254.170.2` from the agent. So:

- **Everything inside a workspace task is agent-controlled**, including any warren helper
  or sidecar in the same task. Nothing goes into a task that the agent must not have.
- **The workspace task role is the control and stays minimal**: EFS ClientMount/ClientWrite
  (or, with EBS storage, `kms:Decrypt` on the secrets key under a `warren:workspace_id`
  encryption context) and the ECS Exec channel. Interim exception, documented: direct Bedrock needs
  `bedrock:InvokeModel*` scoped to exact model/inference-profile ARNs in one region, as a
  separately named statement to remove when the model proxy lands.
- Per-workspace secrets live only in warren's encrypted SQLite store. Just before each
  RunTask the control plane writes the template-declared ones the owner has into the
  workspace's own EFS access point (`.warren/`, outside the checkout), through its root
  mount; git-init and the entrypoint read and delete their file before any workspace
  process starts. Never RunTask overrides (recorded by DescribeTasks/CloudTrail), never the
  task definition, which therefore holds nothing per-owner and can be shared by its cache.
  The access point maps everyone to uid 1000, so the file's protection is timing, not
  permissions: it is on the (encrypted) volume only between the write and container start.
- EBS storage (`WARREN_ECS_WORKSPACE_STORAGE=ebs` or a template's `ecs_storage: ebs`) has
  no volume to write to before the task starts. There, each container's secrets travel as
  an AES-256-GCM envelope in its own RunTask override (`WARREN_SECRETS_BLOB`). The data
  key is wrapped by KMS under the context `warren:workspace_id`/`warren:container`. The
  control plane can only `GenerateDataKey`; the task role `Decrypt`s it in the
  entrypoint/git-init before any workspace process runs, and the blob is unset.
  DescribeTasks and CloudTrail see ciphertext only. The task role is shared, so IAM can't
  stop one workspace from decrypting another's envelope; that needs the envelope, which
  only DescribeTasks/CloudTrail readers have. A workspace's volume is snapshotted on every
  stop and deleted, with all its snapshots, on destroy.
- Clone credentials are given only to the `git-init` container (#29). It copies them into
  private files, re-execs with an empty environment, clones into a private dir, deletes the
  credentials, and only then touches the volume. The workspace container depends on it
  finishing and never receives them.
- EBS bare seeds (`WARREN_ECS_EBS_BARE_SEEDS`, off by default): each owner has one EBS
  seed snapshot of bare mirrors of the repos their EBS workspaces use, and a new workspace
  volume may be created from it. Only the control plane chooses a launch's `snapshotId`,
  and only from the seed row of the workspace's authenticated owner, re-checked against
  the snapshot's own tags (owner, server, `warren.storage=ebs-seed`); a workspace never
  sees another owner's seed, and workspace tasks have no EC2 permissions. A seed is only
  written by that owner's seed-refresh task (git-init container only, never mounted by a
  workspace), fetching with that owner's own credentials, so it holds only what the
  owner's PAT could fetch; nothing is shared across owners and a workspace's stop
  snapshots stay workspace snapshots. git-init uses a seed only on a launch the control
  plane marked as restored from it (`WARREN_GIT_SEEDED=1`) and only if the volume holds
  nothing but the seed, i.e. before any workspace process has run on it. It then rewrites
  each bare's config, refuses symlinks, alternates and a foreign remote, clones locally
  into a repo whose config it wrote, and fetches the delta with the credentials (the one
  credentialed operation on volume content, and that content is seed-task-written).
  The seed is deleted from the volume before the workspace container starts; a bad seed
  falls back to a network clone. The control plane's `git ls-remote` (to skip unchanged
  refreshes) passes the PAT only through `GIT_ASKPASS` in the child environment.
- `git_write: true` templates (T19) get GitHub write tokens in the workspace container.
  The launch's tokens ride with that container's launch-time secrets (EBS envelope / EFS
  file; podman: `put_archive`). Refreshes on ECS go over ECS Exec, and the channel was
  chosen so the token is never in a record: the control plane seals the new tokens into
  a fresh KMS envelope (same context as the launch envelope, `container=workspace`) and
  only that ciphertext is the ExecuteCommand `command`, which CloudTrail records. Nothing
  is written to the session's stdin, so the TTY (ECS Exec always allocates one) has
  nothing to echo, and `warren-git-token` (root, decrypts with the task role) prints only
  a count, so the ECS Exec session log in CloudWatch holds no token either. Piping the
  token over stdin was rejected: the session log records the PTY stream, echo included,
  and there's no way to turn echo off before the first byte arrives. Needs
  `WARREN_ECS_SECRETS_KMS_KEY_ID` on ECS whatever the storage mode.
- No plan should add an in-task bwrap/nsjail layer. Runtime detection on Fargate is
  GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring plus CloudTrail alerting on the task role (AF1, deployment
  IaC, deferred).

## Threats and controls

| # | Threat | Control | Where |
|---|---|---|---|
| T1 | Config `mount:` path (absolute, `..`, symlinked parent) makes warren delete or link outside the workspace | `FolderSpec` validator accepts only normalised relative paths; containment re-checked after `realpath` right before every `rmtree`/symlink, so old persisted specs are covered too | #31 |
| T2 | Agent rewrites its own sandbox policy for the next connect | nsjail config and seatbelt profile live in warren's out-of-tree state dir and are regenerated from the persisted spec on each connect; profiles are parameter-based, not string-built (#34) | #31, #34 |
| T3 | Agent plants `.git` config/hooks (`core.fsmonitor`, hooks, `ext::`) that host-side git then runs | One shared safe-git helper pins `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, `core.fsmonitor=false`, `protocol.ext.allow=never` on every host-side git call; `.git` rename protection in the sandbox (AE1); `git-materialize.sh` uses the same pins, rewrites reused bare-store config from scratch, and never fetches into agent-written repos | #31, #34, #29 |
| T4 | PATH hijack of the sandbox binary itself | `sandbox-exec`/`bwrap`/`nsjail` invoked by absolute path (AE2) | #34 |
| T5 | Workspace gets every project secret, not just its template's | Local provisioning filters `secrets.env` to the template's declared names and errors on missing ones; ECS delivers only the declared names, as a file on the workspace's own volume (EBS: a per-container KMS envelope) | #31, #29 |
| T6 | Host `~/.claude/settings.json` env (API keys etc.) leaks into workspaces and rc files | Implicit import removed; env comes only from template `env:` / `secrets`; `~/.claude` grants scoped per harness, mostly read-only | #31, #34, #36 |
| T7 | Agent steals the SSO token and mints credentials for every AWS account/role | AWS `export` mode: host runs `aws configure export-credentials` for the template's one profile and serves it over a local endpoint; no `~/.aws/sso` grant. `sso` mode only for interactive local `kind: user` workspaces, with a warning | #34 |
| T8 | Long-lived GitHub PAT in the workspace is exfiltrated | GitHub App installation tokens per workspace, limited to the template's repos and `contents`/`pull_requests`, 1-hour lifetime, delivered by a git credential helper (not env, URLs or `.git/config`); local issuer (OS keychain) or control-plane issuer (Secrets Manager, unreadable by the task role) | #33 |
| T9 | git-crypt key decrypts history or leaves with the agent | `git_crypt: none` by default; `unlock_and_drop` unlocks in materialization and deletes the key before the harness starts | #33 |
| T10 | One user acts on another user's workspace (connect, exec, destroy, token, git credentials) | Every workspace route checks the server-resolved owner (`403 not workspace owner`); agents use workspace-scoped machine tokens that can't touch other workspaces; token TTL and admin revoke | #33, earlier PRs |
| T11 | Workspace task assumes the control plane's IAM role or reaches orchestration APIs | Separate, minimal workspace task role; control-plane role not passable to workspaces (#3) | #3 |
| T12 | Clone over SSH is MITM'd | GitHub host keys baked into ecs-base (`ssh_known_hosts`), unknown host keys fail closed; weekly check against `api.github.com/meta` | #29, this PR |
| T13 | Unreviewed harness upgrade changes agent behaviour, or a tampered binary | Harness versions pinned exactly in every Dockerfile; ECS dev base verifies the binary against Anthropic's GPG-signed release manifest, the AWS CLI v2 zip against AWS's PGP signature (key checked in, fingerprint asserted) and the Node.js tarball against a pinned sha256; ecs-base (Debian since 1.5.0) builds su-exec from a sha256-pinned upstream tarball; base images pinned by digest; weekly bump PRs gated on smoke tests | this PR |
| T14 | Duplicate or orphaned tasks keep running (cost, stale access) | Transitional ECS states map to `starting`; idempotent `RunTask` with `clientToken`; orphan sweep; `delete()` purges EFS data | #30 |
| T15 | Repudiation: no record of who issued a secret or token | Structured `event="audit"` records for secret write/delete/delivery, provision/connect/destroy, credential issuance; caller identity from the server, never the client; secret values rejected as fields | #33 |
| T17 | An agent driving warren over MCP provisions, execs in or destroys workspaces beyond its own | `/mcp` sits behind the same AuthMiddleware; each tool calls the REST handler (owner check, audit). A workspace-scoped token's tools only take its own workspace id; only a reservation token (`POST /workspace-tokens`) can provision, exactly once, the id the server chose for it. After a single-turn completion the token can only read status/events and make the idempotent destroy call. Exec output and time are capped (`WARREN_EXEC_MAX_*`) | this PR |
| T18 | Agent steals the operator's Claude login, or uses it to bypass a template meant to run on Bedrock | The sandbox can't read the Keychain or host `~/.claude`. In subscription mode, warren copies in the access token without the refresh token on each connect, so a stolen copy expires and a sandbox refresh can't rotate the host's token. In Bedrock mode (`model_access: bedrock`, or `CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK`/`ANTHROPIC_BEDROCK_BASE_URL` in env), warren reads no Keychain item, deletes any earlier copy, rejects a `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` secret, and only connects with the workspace's own AWS credentials (`export` endpoint locally, task role on ECS) | this PR |
| T19 | An agent steered by untrusted input (issue, PR comment, dependency, web page) pushes malicious commits, rewrites branches or opens PRs with its `git_write` token | Opt-in per template (`git_write: true`), rejected at provision unless the deployment has a GitHub App issuer. The token covers only the template's writable `git-worktree` github.com repos, with exactly `contents: write` + `pull_requests: write` (never administration, workflows or secrets). 1-hour GitHub lifetime, re-minted 10 minutes before expiry and the old one revoked; revoked (`DELETE /installation/token`) and its file removed on stop/destroy; mint/refresh/revoke audited without the token. Stored 0600 under `/run/warren-git` in the container (never env, URLs, `.git/config` or the volume). **Operators must protect `main`** (and release branches): require pull-request reviews, disallow force-push and deletion, so the agent's pushes land only on its own `warren/...` branches and reach `main` through human review | git/write_tokens.py |
| T20 | An sshd in a workspace (opt-in `warren-ecs-ssh-base` / `warren-docker-ssh-base`, for editor remoting) becomes a network entry point, a way past ECS Exec's IAM check, or a root foothold | sshd listens on the task's `127.0.0.1:2222` only (no port mapping, inbound rule or load balancer); the only path to it is an SSM port-forward to the task, which needs `ssm:StartSession` on that task, the permission ECS Exec already needs (and that already gives a root shell), so it admits no one ECS Exec doesn't. sshd runs as uid 1000, started after the entrypoint's privilege drop, so a compromise of it yields the workload's own privileges. Public keys only (`AuthenticationMethods publickey`, no password/keyboard-interactive/PAM/host-based/GSSAPI), `AllowUsers warren`, `PermitRootLogin no`, local TCP forwarding only (no remote/Unix-socket/tun forwarding), no X11 or agent forwarding unless a derived image opts in. No host key in the image: one per workspace, generated on its volume. Authorized keys come from the owner's own secret (or the template's plain env), never RunTask overrides. Asserted with `sshd -T` and live logins by the image smoke test ([workspace-ssh.md](workspace-ssh.md)). The podman counterpart, opt-in `warren-docker-ssh-base`, carries the same sshd hardening and adds no server path: the server publishes its port 22 on the host's loopback only with `WARREN_SSH_ACCESS_ENABLED=true`, the key is the owner-gated `POST /workspaces/{id}/connect` mint (one per workspace, emptied on stop/destroy), and `warren ssh-config` keeps that private key client-side in `~/.warren/ssh/` (owner-only, replaced and invalidated by the next mint). Its sshd runs as container root (two login users), with root logins refused; container root is the agent's own identity there, so this grants nothing new | this PR |
| T21 | A workspace secret named like a process or credential-endpoint variable (`LD_PRELOAD`, `PATH`, `BASH_ENV`, `ENV`, `GIT_*`, `AWS_CONTAINER_CREDENTIALS_FULL_URI`, `AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_KMS`, `WARREN_*`) is exported by the root entrypoint, giving code execution in the task or redirecting the decrypt helper | One shared reserved-name denylist (`secrets/names.py`) enforced by the entrypoint/loaders and as a 422 on `PUT /secrets` | #85 |
| T22 | EBS volumes, snapshots and bare seeds are tagged and swept by `warren.server_instance`; a hostname-default `server_id` orphans them on redeploy and lets two servers sweep each other's resources | `WARREN_SERVER_ID` must be set explicitly (and differ from the hostname) when EBS storage or bare seeds are enabled; settings validation fails at startup naming the fix | round 2 |
| T24 | A browser session token is stolen (XSS, local process, proxy state) and used with full API power | Web-session tokens are a distinct scope with an allow-list that excludes exec, connect, git credentials, token minting and admin; short TTL with hard cap; revoke on disable. Tokens never reach page JS (proxy holds them) | design, not yet built |
| T25 | Cross-site request forges a state-changing call through the proxy | Server stays bearer-only (no cookie auth); proxy cookie is HttpOnly SameSite=Strict with Host/Origin checks; `X-Warren-Client` header on web mutations; no CORS | design, proxy branch `feat-proxy-hardening` |
| T26 | Agent-controlled text (transcripts, run results, git errors, ref names, template text) executes script or spoofs UI in the SPA | Text-only rendering, ANSI allow-list, no auto-linking, strict CSP (no inline script, no eval, `frame-ancestors 'none'`, Trusted Types) | design, not yet built |
| T27 | Admin session turns into impersonation of every owner | Admin routes denied to web scope; audit events for admin mint, disable and revoke; decision on `/runs` admin bypass | design, not yet built |
| T16 | Data exfiltration over the network | **Observed, not blocked.** Podman egress byte counters feed the idle reaper only (D-05). Fail-closed egress (NET-01) is future work; see residual risks | — |
| T23 | Owner-registered notification webhook points the control plane at internal services (SSRF), including by DNS rebinding | HTTPS-only URL shape with no credentials. Per delivery the host is resolved **once** and every returned address must be globally routable (loopback, private, link-local incl. `169.254.169.254`, multicast, reserved, ULA and the IPv4-mapped/6to4/NAT64/Teredo forms are refused). The destination is now **pinned**: the POST connects to one of those validated IPs while the `Host` header, TLS SNI and certificate verification stay on the original host name, so a second DNS answer can't redirect it. Redirects are off, no connection is reused across host names, owner URLs are limited to `WARREN_NOTIFY_ALLOWED_PORTS` (default `443`), and the URL is never logged. The operator's server-wide URL is trusted and not restricted | notify/targets.py, notify/dispatcher.py |

## Known residual risks

1. **Seatbelt is not an isolation boundary.** See the runtime table. Use podman or ECS for
   untrusted work.
2. **Open egress everywhere except nsjail.** An agent can send anything it can read
   (checkout, scoped secrets, its own tokens) to any host. Controls limit *what* it can
   read and for how long tokens work, not where it can send them.
3. **ECS credentials endpoint.** `169.254.170.2` is reachable from the agent, so it holds
   the task role's credentials (and Bedrock access while the interim direct-Bedrock path is in use).
   Mitigated only by keeping the role minimal.
4. **Vendor cloud handoff.** Some harnesses can hand a task to the vendor's own cloud
   (for example Cursor's `&` background/cloud agent). The code and prompt then leave
   warren's boundary entirely, with the vendor's credentials and network. Warren can't
   sandbox that; harness profiles (AG1) should disable or refuse such modes where the
   harness allows it.
5. **Symlink races (TOCTOU).** The workspace-deletion containment check runs immediately before `rmtree`
   and symlink creation, but a workspace process running concurrently can still swap a
   path component between the check and the operation, on any object warren operates on
   in a workspace-writable tree. Destroy-time operations should run when the workspace is
   stopped, and `*at()`/`O_NOFOLLOW` style operations would close the gap.
6. **Host-level reachability for docker/podman.** Workspaces can't reach each other's
   container IPs, published SSH or template ports, or services the host binds on
   `127.0.0.1` (integration-tested for rootless podman with slirp4netns via the gateway,
   `host.containers.internal`, `10.0.2.2`, `169.254.1.2` and the host's own IP). What
   remains:
   - Anything the host listens on at a non-loopback address (`0.0.0.0`, its LAN IP, the
     docker bridge gateway) is reachable from workspaces like from any LAN client. That
     includes other containers' ports published on `0.0.0.0` and, if
     `WARREN_SSH_PUBLISH_HOST` is set to a non-loopback address, other workspaces' SSH
     ports (the per-workspace key is then the only barrier). Bind host services to
     loopback or firewall them from the container subnets.
   - slirp4netns keeps host loopback closed by default; `allow_host_loopback=true`
     reopens it. **pasta doesn't, at least on podman 6.0.1 with default options:**
     `169.254.1.2` (`host.containers.internal`) reached host loopback from a workspace,
     so other workspaces' published SSH and template ports were reachable. The server now
     enforces this: it refuses to start on rootless podman whose `podman info` reports
     pasta (or, on podman 5+, doesn't report a helper) unless `WARREN_PODMAN_ALLOW_PASTA=1`
     is set; with the override it logs `podman_pasta_host_loopback` at startup and the
     risk is accepted. pasta `--map-gw`/`-T` open more still.
   - Docker Desktop and colima route `host.docker.internal` (colima: `192.168.5.2`) to
     the desktop host, whose `localhost` services, including ports the VM forwards, are
     then reachable from workspaces. Warren can't close that from inside the VM.
   - Templates' `docker_networks` are shared by design.
   Each podman workspace uses one /24 from the netavark subnet pool, which bounds
   concurrent podman workspaces per host (about 255 with the default pool).
7. **Same kernel for bwrap, nsjail, docker and podman.** A kernel exploit escapes all of
   them; only Fargate's micro-VM is a separate kernel.
8. **Deployment-wide GitHub App repo allow-list** (`WARREN_GITHUB_APP_ALLOWED_REPOS`): any
   authenticated user can mint tokens for any listed repo. One server per trust boundary.
9. **Local GitHub App issuer key exposure.** Whoever holds the key mints for every repo the
   App is installed on. Acceptable for a single operator; team deployments use the
   control-plane issuer only.
10. **Runtime threat detection is not in place** (AF1, deferred).
11. **Images built FROM a bumped base by digest** (`warren-agent-base`,
    `warren-ecs-agent-base`, `warren-docker-ssh-base`, and `warren-ecs-ssh-base` once its dev-base pin is a digest) lag a harness bump until the base is republished and the digest
    updated.
12. **`warren proxy` listeners.** Mappings of kind `tcp` (the default) are still a loopback byte
    relay with no authentication of their own: any process or user on the operator's machine that
    can connect to `127.0.0.1:<port>` reaches that workspace port over the operator's SSM session
    or SSH forward, and a DNS-rebinding page can reach a web app on it. On a podman/docker server
    host, any local process or user can likewise reach a workspace's published template ports on
    `127.0.0.1`. Whatever the service enforces (for example T3 Code's pairing) is the only check;
    don't run the proxy on a shared host. Mitigated for `kind: http` mappings (template
    `protocol: http`): exact loopback `Host` check (DNS rebinding), a one-time start link exchanged
    for an `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Strict` session cookie held server-side with an idle expiry (other
    local processes and pages), same-origin enforcement with no CORS (cross-site requests and
    WebSockets), strict request parsing (smuggling) and size, time and connection limits. Residual
    for `http`: a process running as the operator can read the terminal or the browser's cookies;
    cookies are shared across ports of `127.0.0.1`; no TLS on loopback. `tcp` mappings now also
    refuse ports held by another process (and `proxy run` fails if a port cannot be bound), write
    `proxy.json` owner-only without following symlinks, and halt if their workspace id changes.
    `--unix` mappings listen on a 0600 socket in a 0700 directory instead of a TCP port, with a
    peer-uid check per connection, so other local users and DNS-rebinding pages cannot reach them;
    residual: any process running as the operator can still connect (file permissions are the only
    authentication, so an `http` mapping on a socket issues no start link or cookie), and
    sockets are not available on Windows.
13. **`git_write` tokens are live inside the workspace.** Until revoked on stop or
    superseded (at most an hour), the agent can push to and open PRs on every repo the
    token covers, and can send the token anywhere (open egress). Only branch protection
    on those repos keeps that from reaching `main`. A refresh revokes the superseded
    token, so a push in flight across a refresh can fail once and needs a retry.
14. **The workspace sshd is agent-controlled** (`warren-ecs-ssh-base`/`warren-docker-ssh-base`,
    T20). It runs as container root on podman (the workload, and the SSH `warren` login, are uid 1000 there under keep-id; root inside is an unprivileged subuid, but it is not the agent's own identity any more) and as uid 1000 on ECS, so the agent can, on ECS, read its host key, add keys to
    `authorized_keys` (still usable only through `ssm:StartSession` on the task, or SSH
    access to the podman host), or
    replace the sshd and see what an SSH session does. An editor session is therefore
    as exposed to the agent as an ECS Exec session is: don't forward your agent
    (off by default) or type credentials into a workspace you don't trust. The ssh
    client is set to publickey only (`warren ssh-config`), so an impostor sshd can't
    prompt for a password. On EBS the host key is in the volume's snapshots.
15. **The shared workspace task role can decrypt any envelope whose ciphertext it holds.**
    ECS secrets travel as KMS envelopes that the workspace task role can open (it must, to
    read its own), and that role is shared across workspaces. Envelope ciphertext appears
    in ECS `RunTask` overrides and in CloudTrail `ExecuteCommand` records, so anyone who
    can read it and act as the task role (including an agent that obtains the role's
    credentials, risk 3) can open it. Treat that ciphertext as sensitive: limit
    `ecs:DescribeTasks` and CloudTrail read access (and the log destinations they feed) to
    people who may already read the secrets.
16. **Write tokens are readable by the agent for their lifetime.** A write-access GitHub
    token file is readable by the workload (uid 1000) while the token is live, at most one
    hour (T19). Revocation on stop/destroy is best effort: if the revoke call fails or the
    server is down, the token stays valid until it expires.
7. **Admin can mint a token for any user.** `POST /admin/users/{id}/tokens` is by design an impersonation primitive and is not yet audited as an event. Treat the admin token as equivalent to every owner's.
8. **Unscoped legacy user tokens remain full API.** Until the web-session scope ships, any token the CLI holds can exec and mint further tokens; a UI must not be exposed before it does.
9. **Rendering agent output in a browser is a new injection surface.** Even with text-only rendering and strict CSP, a bug in the ANSI or link handling is reachable by anything the agent can print.
